  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Do crickets chirp with their legs?",
      "a": "No. Crickets chirp by rubbing their two front wings together. Grasshoppers are the ones that often rub a back leg against a wing.",
      "asks": [
        "How do crickets make noise?",
        "Do crickets rub their legs?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is stridulation?",
      "a": "Stridulation is making sound by rubbing one body part against another, like a scraper across a file of tiny ridges. Crickets, grasshoppers and some beetles and ants do it.",
      "asks": [
        "What does stridulate mean?",
        "Which animals stridulate?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are crickets so loud for their size?",
      "a": "The harp, a thin, stiff patch on the wing, resonates at the same rate as the tooth clicks, turning many small clicks into one strong, pure note. Some crickets also call from burrows that amplify the sound.",
      "asks": [
        "How can a small insect be so loud?",
        "What does the cricket harp do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do crickets chirp?",
      "a": "Male crickets chirp mainly to attract females and warn off rival males. Different species have different songs, so females can find males of their own kind.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do only male crickets chirp?",
        "What does a cricket chirp mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where are a cricket's ears?",
      "a": "A cricket's ears are on its front legs, just below the knees. Each is a small drum-like membrane that picks up the chirps of other crickets.",
      "asks": [
        "Do crickets have ears?",
        "How do crickets hear?"
      ]
    }
  ],
